# LiveKit 1.13.6 (image livekit/livekit-server:v1.13.6) environment schema
# (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# Read this first: livekit-server is a Go binary and its configuration surface
# is a YAML document, not a list of environment variables. Upstream ships no
# `.env.example` and publishes no env-var reference table, because there is
# nothing to reference: nearly every knob is a YAML path in config-sample.yaml.
#
# Three mechanisms turn that YAML into environment at this version:
#
#   1. `LIVEKIT_CONFIG` carries the WHOLE config file as its value. It is the
#      env source of the `--config-body` flag (cmd/server/main.go baseFlags),
#      and it is what this template uses, which is why the stack needs no bind
#      mount, and why most settings below do not exist as variables at all.
#   2. Every SCALAR path in the config struct also gets a hidden CLI flag whose
#      env twin is `LIVEKIT_` + the YAML path uppercased with `.` turned into
#      `_` (pkg/config/config.go, GenerateCLIFlags): `rtc.tcp_port` becomes
#      `LIVEKIT_RTC_TCP_PORT`, `logging.level` becomes `LIVEKIT_LOGGING_LEVEL`.
#      Maps, slices and structs are explicitly skipped (`// TODO` arms of that
#      switch), so `keys`, `rtc.stun_servers`, `rtc.turn_servers`,
#      `webhook.urls`, `room.enabled_codecs` and `node_selector.regions` have
#      NO env twin and can only be set inside the YAML blob.
#   3. A handful of hand-written flags whose env names do NOT follow that rule:
#      LIVEKIT_KEYS, LIVEKIT_REGION, LIVEKIT_TURN_CERT, LIVEKIT_TURN_KEY, and
#      the unprefixed NODE_IP, UDP_PORT, REDIS_HOST, REDIS_PASSWORD.
#
# Precedence (pkg/config/config.go, NewConfig): built-in defaults, then the
# YAML from LIVEKIT_CONFIG, then the variables above. An environment variable
# always beats the compose's YAML.
#
# THE TRAP, verified on this image: "set to empty" is not "unset". Booting
# v1.13.6 with a YAML of `rtc: {use_external_ip: true, tcp_port: 7881}` plus
# `LIVEKIT_RTC_USE_EXTERNAL_IP=` and `LIVEKIT_RTC_TCP_PORT=` present but blank
# starts a server with external-IP discovery OFF and TCP port 0, the exact
# "clients connect, negotiate, then sit in silence" failure the template's
# compose comment is about. Removing the two blank variables restores STUN
# discovery and `rtc.portTCP: 7881`. So the port/IP knobs below carry this
# stack's effective values rather than blanks: blanking one in the editor does
# not fall back to the YAML, it overrides the YAML with a zero.
#
# Source, at the v1.13.6 tag: cmd/server/main.go (baseFlags and the dev-mode
# branch), pkg/config/config.go (GenerateCLIFlags, updateFromCLI, NewConfig,
# DefaultConfig), pkg/service/server.go (prometheus) and pkg/service/turn.go,
# cross-checked against config-sample.yaml at the same tag. Every name here was
# then confirmed by booting livekit/livekit-server:v1.13.6 with it set and
# reading the startup log.
#
# SECURITY: the important part. A LiveKit API key/secret pair is not a
# password to one account: it signs the JWTs that grant access to EVERY room on
# the server. Anyone holding the pair can mint a token to join, publish to,
# subscribe to, moderate, record or delete any room, and can call the whole
# server API. Treat the pair like a signing key, never ship it to browsers, and
# mint short-lived per-participant tokens from your own backend instead.
#
# Upstream's development mode (the `--dev` flag, or its env twin
# LIVEKIT_DEVELOPMENT below) hands out a placeholder pair (API key `devkey`,
# secret `secret`) that is printed in every LiveKit quickstart on the
# internet. Those two strings must never reach a reachable deployment: they are
# public knowledge, so they are equivalent to no authentication at all. This
# stack sets real keys through LIVEKIT_KEYS, and the server refuses to start
# without them ("one of key-file or keys must be provided", reproduced on
# v1.13.6), so the placeholder is only ever used if someone turns development
# mode on.
#
# Two more knobs here weaken the deployment and are called out on their items:
# LIVEKIT_DEVELOPMENT (debug logging, /debug/pprof, placeholder keys, loopback
# binding) and LIVEKIT_TURN_EXTERNAL_TLS (TURN advertises TLS while speaking
# plaintext, safe only behind a TLS-terminating L4 load balancer). The
# prometheus endpoint is unauthenticated unless both username and password are
# set.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Deploying LiveKit", https://docs.livekit.io/transport/self-hosting/deployment/)
# ---

# ─── API keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The key half of the single API key/secret pair this server trusts. It is the
# `iss` claim of every access token, so it is not secret in the way the secret
# is, but it is generated per deployment and pairs with the secret below, so
# it is masked here too. The compose feeds it into LIVEKIT_KEYS; changing it
# invalidates every token already minted against the old pair.
# @required @sensitive
# @docs("Access tokens & grants", https://docs.livekit.io/frontends/reference/tokens-grants/)
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=

# The signing secret for that key. Whoever holds it can sign a token for any
# room with any grant (join, publish, subscribe, record, moderate, destroy)
# and can call the server API directly. It never leaves the server side: your
# own backend uses it to mint short-lived participant tokens. Generate it,
# never reuse it across deployments, and never use upstream's `secret`.
# @required @sensitive
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=

# How the server actually receives the pair: one `key: secret` line per pair,
# parsed as YAML (cmd/server/main.go `--keys`, pkg/config/config.go
# unmarshalKeys). The space after the colon is mandatory; without it the
# server exits "Could not parse keys, it needs to be exactly, \"key: secret\"".
# The compose builds this from the two variables above, deliberately outside
# LIVEKIT_CONFIG so the secret is masked in the editor instead of being buried
# in a config blob that reads as plain text. With neither this nor a key file
# set, v1.13.6 refuses to boot: "one of key-file or keys must be provided".
# @required @sensitive
LIVEKIT_KEYS=${LIVEKIT_API_KEY}: ${LIVEKIT_API_SECRET}

# Alternative to the above: path INSIDE the container to a YAML file of
# `key: secret` lines, for operators who mount their keys as a Docker or
# Kubernetes secret rather than passing them as environment. Only one of the
# two is needed; the file is read at boot and a missing path aborts startup
# ("stat <path>: no such file or directory"). Leave empty for this stack.
LIVEKIT_KEY_FILE=

# ─── Server configuration ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The entire LiveKit config file, as YAML, in one variable. The compose already
# supplies it (signalling port, log level, the RTC ports, UDP mux and external
# IP discovery, and the bundled Redis address), so it is empty here rather than
# duplicated: this is a value the template owns, and the place to change any
# setting that has no variable of its own (webhooks, room defaults, limits,
# codecs, STUN/TURN server lists, node selector). Parsing is STRICT: an unknown
# or misspelled YAML key aborts startup with "could not parse config".
# @docs("Config sample (every YAML key, annotated)", https://github.com/livekit/livekit/blob/v1.13.6/config-sample.yaml)
LIVEKIT_CONFIG=

# TCP port for signalling (WebSocket) and the server APIs. This is what the
# platform routes public traffic to. It matches `port: 7880` in the compose's
# YAML and the exposed container port; changing it here alone breaks routing,
# and blanking it sets the port to 0.
# @type=port
LIVEKIT_PORT=7880

# Server log level: debug, info, warn or error. `logging.level` is the current
# path; the compose's YAML still uses the older top-level `log_level: info`,
# which pkg/config/config.go copies into `logging.level` while marking it
# deprecated. Empty leaves the compose's `info` in place. Debug is very
# chatty (it logs per-participant signalling) and is not a privacy-neutral
# setting on a production media server.
# @type=enum(debug, info, warn, error)
LIVEKIT_LOGGING_LEVEL=

# Deprecated twin of the above (the top-level `log_level` YAML key). Set one or
# the other, not both; this one is only here because it is what the compose's
# YAML and most older LiveKit examples use.
# @type=enum(debug, info, warn, error)
LIVEKIT_LOG_LEVEL=

# Development mode, the env twin of the `--dev` flag. NEVER set this on a
# deployed stack. On v1.13.6 it turns on debug logging and the /debug/pprof
# handlers, and when no keys are configured it invents the publicly known
# placeholder pair (`devkey` / `secret`) and logs it, which is the same as
# running with no authentication; it also binds to loopback only, so the stack
# stops answering from outside anyway.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Running LiveKit locally (what --dev does)", https://docs.livekit.io/transport/self-hosting/local/)
LIVEKIT_DEVELOPMENT=false

# ─── WebRTC media ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ICE/TCP port, used when a client cannot get UDP through. It must be reachable
# directly on the node; it cannot sit behind a load balancer or TLS. Mirrors
# `rtc.tcp_port: 7881` in the compose's YAML and the exposed 7881; blanking it
# sets it to 0 and disables the TCP fallback path.
# @type=port
# @docs("Ports and firewall", https://docs.livekit.io/transport/self-hosting/ports-firewall/)
LIVEKIT_RTC_TCP_PORT=7881

# UDP mux port: every participant's media multiplexed onto ONE UDP port instead
# of the default 50000-60000 range (two ports per participant), which is what
# makes this deployable through a single published port. Mirrors
# `rtc.udp_port: 7882` in the compose's YAML and the exposed 7882/udp. Note the
# odd name: this one is a hand-written flag, so it is `UDP_PORT`, not
# `LIVEKIT_RTC_UDP_PORT`. It accepts a range too ("7882-7892").
UDP_PORT=7882

# Discover the node's public address over STUN at boot and advertise that as
# the ICE candidate. Inside a container the address the server can see is a
# private one, so with this off clients connect, negotiate and then hear
# nothing. Mirrors `rtc.use_external_ip: true` in the compose's YAML. Note the
# built-in default is false, so this must stay true (or be replaced by NODE_IP)
# for media to work.
# @type=boolean
LIVEKIT_RTC_USE_EXTERNAL_IP=true

# Explicit public IP to advertise instead of discovering one, for hosts where
# STUN returns the wrong address (multiple NATs, some VPS providers). Only
# takes effect when external-IP discovery is off. Empty means "discover it".
NODE_IP=

# ─── Redis ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# host:port of the Redis that holds node and room registrations. The compose
# already points the server at the bundled Redis inside LIVEKIT_CONFIG
# (`redis.address: ${{stack.redis.HOST}}:6379`), so leave this empty. Setting
# it blank is not neutral, it would blank the address and drop the server back
# to single-node routing. Fill it only to point at an external Redis instead,
# which is also what makes multi-node and the egress/ingress/SIP add-ons work.
# @docs("Distributed multi-region setup", https://docs.livekit.io/transport/self-hosting/distributed/)
REDIS_HOST=

# Password for that Redis. The bundled one runs on the stack's private network
# with no password, so this stays empty unless you repoint REDIS_HOST at an
# authenticated server. Redis holds live routing state, not media.
# @sensitive
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# ─── Embedded TURN server (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────

# Turns on LiveKit's built-in TURN server, for clients on networks that block
# everything except 443. Off by default, and this template does not publish the
# TURN ports, so enabling it means also exposing them and pointing a hostname
# at the node. If you enable it without setting at least one of the two ports
# below, startup fails with "invalid TURN ports" (the config sample's claim of
# 3478/5349 defaults does not hold: DefaultConfig leaves both at 0).
# @type=boolean
# @docs("TURN section of the annotated config sample", https://github.com/livekit/livekit/blob/v1.13.6/config-sample.yaml)
LIVEKIT_TURN_ENABLED=false

# Hostname clients reach the TURN server on. It must match the CN/SAN of the
# TLS certificate used for TURN/TLS, otherwise clients reject the relay.
LIVEKIT_TURN_DOMAIN=

# TURN/TLS port. 443 is the point of the exercise on restrictive networks; the
# upstream sample suggests 5349 when something else already owns 443.
# @type=port
LIVEKIT_TURN_TLS_PORT=

# TURN/UDP port. Only 53, 80 and 443 are permitted below 1024; 3478 is the
# standard choice otherwise.
# @type=port
LIVEKIT_TURN_UDP_PORT=

# Set only when an L4 load balancer terminates TLS in front of TURN: the server
# then speaks PLAINTEXT on the TLS port while still advertising it as a
# TURN/TLS candidate. Turning this on without that terminator in front means
# relayed traffic is advertised as encrypted and is not.
# @type=boolean
LIVEKIT_TURN_EXTERNAL_TLS=false

# Path inside the container to the TLS certificate (PEM) for TURN/TLS, when
# LiveKit terminates TLS itself rather than a load balancer. Mount the file;
# this is a path, not the certificate body.
LIVEKIT_TURN_CERT=

# Path inside the container to the matching private key (PEM). Required
# together with the certificate above.
# @sensitive
LIVEKIT_TURN_KEY=

# ─── Metrics ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Port for a Prometheus /metrics endpoint on its own listener, separate from
# the signalling port. Empty (0) disables it. The template does not publish
# this port; expose it only to your scraper. Note the metrics expose room,
# participant and track counts, so it is not a public endpoint.
# @type=port
LIVEKIT_PROMETHEUS_PORT=

# Basic-auth username for that endpoint. On v1.13.6 the server FAILS TO START
# if exactly one of username/password is set ("prometheus username or password
# is set but not both"), a deliberate change in this release, so a half-filled
# pair can no longer silently leave metrics open. Both empty means the endpoint
# is served unauthenticated.
LIVEKIT_PROMETHEUS_USERNAME=

# Basic-auth password for the metrics endpoint. Set together with the username
# or leave both empty.
# @sensitive
LIVEKIT_PROMETHEUS_PASSWORD=

# ─── Cluster ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Name of this node's region, used only by the `regionaware` node selector in a
# multi-node deployment (the region coordinates themselves live in the YAML,
# under `node_selector.regions`). Irrelevant for a single-node stack.
LIVEKIT_REGION=
